**=================================================================================================
** Winkler L-panel, variant 3 of 3: blockamg (as variant 2) plus live h-adaptivity. Refinement runs
** ahead of the crack: every concrete element whose plastic hardening variable alphaP exceeds 0.1 is
** split into 2x2x2 children (one level), in batches of at least 20 marked elements (fewer rebuilds of
** the equation system); the hanging nodes of the refined elements are constrained. Refining while the
** elements are still nearly elastic keeps the transferred state close to equilibrium -- refining only
** at alphaP > 0.8, inside already softening elements, disturbs it enough to force cutbacks.
** The finer crack band gives a lower load-displacement curve than variants 1 and 2.
** Units, geometry, boundary conditions and material: model.inp. How to run: README.md.
**=================================================================================================
*include, input=model.inp

*job, name=WinklerL_blockamg_amr, domain=3d
*solver, solver=NISTParallel, name=theSolver
linsolver=blockamg
** every blockamg setting, defaults included -- explained in README.md
linsolverConfigFile=blockamg.json
** condense the hanging-node constraints with AMGCL's threaded products (faster; off by default)
useAmgclMPCCondensation=True

*modelModifier, type=hAdaptivity, name=amr
** mark every element whose largest alphaP (the fieldOutput of model.inp) exceeds 0.1
>>marker, type=fieldOutput, fieldOutput=alphaP, operator='>', threshold=0.1
** octree roots: all elements, steel included, so refinement next to the steel blocks stays conforming
refineElSet=all
** refine once (maxLevel), each marked element into 2 x 2 x 2 children (splitFactor)
maxLevel=1
splitFactor=2
** collect marks until at least 20 elements are marked, then refine them in one pass
minMarkedElements=20

*fieldOutput
** reaction force (sum) and displacement (mean) of the load line, y component, as histories, exported to CSV
>>perNode, name=RF, nSet=setLoad, field=displacement, result=P, f(x)='np.sum(x[:,1])', saveHistory=True, export=RF_blockamg_amr
>>perNode, name=U, nSet=setLoad, field=displacement, result=U, f(x)='np.mean(x[:,1])', saveHistory=True, export=U_blockamg_amr

** prints the reaction force after every increment
*output, type=monitor, name=monitor
fieldOutput=RF

** EnSight results for ParaView or pyvista: WinklerL_blockamg_amr.case
*output, type=ensight, name=WinklerL_blockamg_amr
>>perNode, fieldOutput=displacement
>>perNode, fieldOutput=nonlocalDamage
>>perElement, fieldOutput=damage
>>perElement, fieldOutput=alphaP
>>configuration, overwrite=yes

** One step over pseudo-time 0..1 (stepLength). The increment adapts between minInc and maxInc (so at
** least 100 increments); an increment that needs more than maxIter Newton iterations is cut back.
*step, solver=theSolver
maxInc=1e-2, minInc=1e-8, maxNumInc=10000, maxIter=25, stepLength=1
** linear predictor: each increment starts from the last converged increment, extrapolated linearly
>>options, name=theSolver, extrapolation=linear
>>dirichlet, name=bottom, nSet=setBottom, field=displacement, 1=0, 2=0, 3=0
>>dirichlet, name=back, nSet=setBack, field=displacement, 3=0
** the load: the load line is raised by 1 mm (u_y = +1) over the step -- the peak load is reached at
** about 0.1 mm, the panel then softens as the crack grows from the re-entrant corner
>>dirichlet, name=load, nSet=setLoad, field=displacement, 2=+1
